-The reward audit identifies a mechanism that suppresses useful repairs: counting temporary duplicates can outweigh quality gains. The rate and zero variants reduce this effect, while simple policies remain competitive in the fixed registry. The calibrated small-count control is close to rate DDQN at the base size, with no significant paired difference. Separate disjoint-replication tests examine fixed-policy behavior and penalty scaling; connected graphs and varied repair risks remain different settings. The reference-preservation results describe the controlled operators, including trusted relation restoration; natural-error safety requires independent labels.
